{"name":"Hearty Hosta","hex":"#96bf83","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hearty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Warm and cordial towards another person.","Energetic, active or eager."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["a term of familiar address and fellowship among sailors."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hearty"},{"term":"hosta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several herbaceous Asiatic plants of the genus Hosta."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hosta"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hosta","blocks":[{"text":"Hosta is a genus of plants commonly known as hostas, plantain lilies and occasionally by the Japanese name gibōshi. Hostas are widely cultivated as shade-tolerant foliage plants. The genus is placed in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Agavoideae, and is native to northeast Asia. Like many \"lilioid monocots\", the genus was once classified in the Liliaceae. The genus was named by Austrian botanist Leopold Trattinnick in 1812, in honor of the Austrian botanist Nicholas Thomas Host. In 1817, the generic name Funkia was used by German botanist Kurt Sprengel in honor of Heinrich Christian Funck, a collector of ferns and alpines. It was later used as a common name and is referenced in some older literature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hosta_Bressingham_Blue.JPG/330px-Hosta_Bressingham_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hosta","wikidata":"Q623347"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=e